.\" realpath.1 -- maux's realpath(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An
.\" original page (this project's own text, not copied from a
.\" historic realpath(1)), so it carries no third-party notice --
.\" MIT like the code, same posture as env.1. Rendered at build
.\" time to build/man/REALPATH.TXT and staged on the play image
.\" as /usr/man/REALPATH: `more /usr/man/REALPATH`.
.TH REALPATH 1 "September 15, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
realpath \- print the absolute, canonical pathname of each operand
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B realpath
[
.B \-E
|
.B \-e
]
.I file
...
.SH DESCRIPTION
For each
.I file
operand,
.I realpath
resolves it against the current directory if it is not already
absolute, folds every
.B .
and
.B ..
component, collapses repeated slashes, and prints the result.
ProDOS has no symbolic links, so none are ever resolved. A
.B ..
above a volume's own root has nowhere to go and is a no-op,
leaving the path at the root.
.PP
A failure on one operand prints a diagnostic to standard error
and continues with the next; the exit status reflects whether
any operand failed.
.TP
.B \-E
Do not treat it as an error if resolving the last component
results in "no such file or directory" -- only the components
before it need exist. This is the default.
.TP
.B \-e
Every component, including the last, must exist.
.PP
If both
.B \-E
and
.B \-e
are given, the last one given takes effect.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/REALPATH  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
pwd(1), dirname(1), basename(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
0 if every operand resolved (and, under
.BR \-e ,
exists in full); 1 if any operand could not be resolved or does
not satisfy
.BR \-e / \-E ;
2 on a usage error.
.SH BUGS
The canonical form is whatever case ProDOS itself stored the
name in, the same as
.IR pwd (1)
prints -- there is no case folding of its own. A pathname that
folds to longer than 64 characters (ProDOS's own pathname limit)
is refused rather than truncated.
